h with studies  looking to quantify some financial costs  of inequality and possible benefits of  overcoming it one new paper puts the  price tag at nearly 23 trillion dollars  in the u.s over the past 30 years the  gaps in labor market outcomes that we we  observe that sideline or under utilize  either way  workers can translate directly into  lower output and conversely closing  those gaps can translate into higher  economic output those economic realities  have helped policymakers refocus the  nation's priorities even the federal  reserve has said its new goal is to  strengthen employment in a way that's  broad-based and inclusive and president  biden has made reducing inequality a  centerpiece of his agenda when we lift  each other up we're all lifted up  you know the corollary is true as well  when any one of us is held down  we're all held back  more and more economic studies in recent  years have proven this  but i don't think you need economic  studies to see the truth our metrics for  success as a nation will continue to  change as they have for hundreds of  years but today the biden administration  and others say shared success is both a  moral and economic obligation and that  it will help make the us stronger and  fairer than ever before  [Music]
 

